Fork deploy files (live updates and pages API)
==============================================

Files in this directory
  docker-compose.override.yaml  fork images and feature flags, layered over the
                                upstream community compose file
  inspect.sh                    read-only pre-deploy inspection (run on the hypervisor)
  fetch-images.sh               downloads the image archive, verifies its sha256, then
                                runs fork/build/verify-load.sh
  test_fetch-images.sh          tests fetch-images.sh (stub verify-load, loopback HTTP)
  test_override.sh              validates the override against the upstream compose file
  test_inspect.sh               tests inspect.sh against stub commands
  RUNBOOK.md                    the deploy procedure, smoke tests and rollback

WARNINGS

1. The community installer (setup.sh, from the upstream release) starts the
   stack with an explicit "-f docker-compose.yaml". Docker Compose only picks up
   docker-compose.override.yaml automatically when NO -f is given, so anything
   started or upgraded through setup.sh (start, restart, upgrade) runs the stock
   images and drops every fork flag WITHOUT any error. The upgrade action also
   re-downloads the stock docker-compose.yaml. Never use setup.sh on a fork
   deployment; use the two-file commands below. After any use of setup.sh, redo
   the image-tag check in RUNBOOK.md.

2. live must stay at ONE replica (leave LIVE_REPLICAS unset or 1). The pages
   API asks live whether a page is open in an editor; that state is held per
   process, so a second replica would answer "not loaded" for pages open on the
   other one and the API could overwrite an open document.

3. LIVE_BASE_URL is set on api ONLY. Do not set it on worker or beat-worker, in
   plane.env, or anywhere else that reaches them: with it set, the copy_s3_object
   task (bgtasks/copy_s3_object.py) starts calling live's convert-document
   endpoint when a page is duplicated. The value must be the direct internal
   URL of the live container (http://live:3000), never the public URL: live
   refuses requests that carry X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Host.

4. LIVE_SERVER_SECRET_KEY must not be the shipped placeholder
   (change-this-key-on-deployment); the pages endpoints answer 503 until it is
   changed. Do not print it. Never run "docker compose config" without -q on
   the server: it prints every secret.

5. CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS must not be passed into live. Browser origins for the
   events socket go in LIVE_EVENTS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS.

Variables (supplied by the operator; nothing host-specific is stored in the repo)
  FORK_N                       fork build number, image tag v1.4.2-live.$FORK_N
  WEB_URL                      public origin (normally already in plane.env)
  LIVE_EVENTS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS  comma-separated origins users open Plane from
                               (scheme://host[:port], for example the values of
                               PUBLIC_URL and LAN_URL)
  PLANE_APP_DIR                the plane-app directory that holds docker-compose.yaml and plane.env

Optional live events limits (set in the live service environment; defaults shown)
  LIVE_EVENTS_MAX_PENDING_SOCKETS       100    sockets that have not authenticated yet
  LIVE_EVENTS_MAX_SOCKETS_PER_ADDRESS   25     sockets per remote address
  LIVE_EVENTS_AUTH_TIMEOUT_MS           8000   hub-side authentication timeout (kept below 20000)
  LIVE_EVENTS_AUTH_FAIL_CACHE_MS        30000  how long a rejected session cookie is refused without a
                                               lookup (0 disables)
  LIVE_EVENTS_TRUSTED_PROXIES           empty  comma-separated IPs or CIDR ranges of reverse proxies whose
                                               X-Forwarded-For is trusted for the per-address limit.
                                               The per-address limit depends on this setting: while it
                                               is empty, sockets from a loopback, private, link-local or
                                               unique-local peer (normally the proxy itself) are not
                                               limited per address and a warning is logged once. Include
                                               CDN edge ranges as well, otherwise the address seen is a
                                               shared edge address.

Optional live events tuning (defaults shown; leave unset to use them)
  LIVE_EVENTS_REVALIDATE_MS             60000   how often each socket's session and project
                                                grants are rechecked (+/-20% jitter per socket)
  LIVE_EVENTS_REVALIDATE_MAX_FAILURES   3       consecutive transient recheck failures before the
                                                socket is closed with code 1013

Live events counters (numbers since process start; no ids, users or addresses)
  GET /health/stats on the live service. It is internal only: read it from inside the
  deploy network (for example with curl from a shell in the live or api container),
  never through the public reverse proxy. A request that carries X-Forwarded-For,
  X-Forwarded-Host, Forwarded or X-Real-IP answers 403 even with the right secret, so
  the shared secret is never sent through the public proxy. The header
  live-server-secret-key must hold the value of LIVE_SERVER_SECRET_KEY (take it from
  the container environment, do not paste it into shell history or tickets). No header
  or a wrong value answers 401; an unset or placeholder secret answers 503. The public
  /health stays minimal and shows no counters. Example, run inside the live container
  (LIVE_PORT is the port the live service listens on):

    curl -s -H "live-server-secret-key: $LIVE_SERVER_SECRET_KEY" "http://localhost:$LIVE_PORT/health/stats"

  Fields: startedAt, uptimeSeconds, eventsEnabled and, when the hub runs, events:
    sockets.open|authenticated|pending   current gauges
    accepted                             sockets that passed the pre-auth checks
    authenticated                        sockets that finished authentication
    rejected.pendingCap|addressCap|authFail|authTimeout|other
    framesSent                           frames written to sockets
    invalid.redisDropped|clientMessages  malformed Redis messages dropped, bad client frames closed
    rateLimitOverflows.inboundMessages   4429 closes for message rate
    rateLimitOverflows.projectEvents     per-project overflows answered with a full refresh
    revalidationCloses.auth4401|unavailable1013

Optional pages API size and time limits (defaults shown; leave unset to use them)
  api service:
  PAGES_API_MAX_HTML_BYTES              262144  largest description_html (decoded bytes) accepted by the
                                                pages API on create and update; larger answers 413
  live service:
  PAGES_REBASE_MAX_HTML_BYTES           524288  largest description_html the rebase route converts;
                                                larger answers 413, which the api passes on
                                                as 413. Keep it at or above the api value
  PAGES_REBASE_TIMEOUT_MS               8000    time budget of one conversion; on expiry the worker thread
                                                is stopped and the route answers 503 (keep it below the api
                                                client timeout of 10 s)
  PAGES_REBASE_MAX_CONCURRENCY          2       conversions (worker threads) at the same time; more are
                                                refused at once with 503 and Retry-After. Do not set it
                                                above the spare CPU cores of the live container (use 1 on
                                                a 2-core host): busy workers compete with the main thread
  PAGES_REBASE_WORKER_MAX_MB            256     heap limit of one worker thread; a worker that exceeds it
                                                is stopped, the request fails (the api answers 503) and
                                                the next request gets a fresh worker. A base document over
                                                about 2 MB of binary state can need more. A
                                                --max-old-space-size in NODE_OPTIONS overrides this limit

Commands (run in $PLANE_APP_DIR; FORK_N must be exported or in plane.env)

  C="docker compose -f docker-compose.yaml -f docker-compose.override.yaml --env-file plane.env"

  $C config -q                  # validate, prints nothing on success
  $C up -d                      # apply (recreates only changed services)
  $C ps --format '{{.Service}} {{.Image}}'    # image-tag check
  $C logs --tail 50 live api

Local validation of the override, no server needed
  fork/deploy/test_override.sh --self-mutate
Uses docker compose, podman compose or docker-compose if present, and always the
python3 + PyYAML checks.

Read-only inspection of the target (on the hypervisor; changes nothing)
  ssh "$PVE_HOST" "PLANE_CTID=$PLANE_CTID PLANE_APP_DIR=$PLANE_APP_DIR bash -s" < fork/deploy/inspect.sh
  add "-- --dry-run" after "bash -s" to print the commands without running them.

The migrator service keeps the stock backend image: the fork adds no database
migrations. If that ever changes, add the migrator to the override.
